<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#llanito">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llanito</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yanito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llanito is the vernacular speech of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, an Andalusian Spanish base mixed heavily with British English through frequent code-switching. It also contains words from Genoese, Maltese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and other languages of the settlers who made up Gibraltar's population. It is a marker of Gibraltarian identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/llanito.jpg" alt="Llanito" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original by Eric Gaba (Sting), label/legend edits by Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gibraltar_map-en-edit2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the word Llanito is uncertain. In Spanish llanito means little flatland, and one view is that it first named the people of La Línea, which lies on the plain and marsh round the Rock. Another theory takes it as a diminutive of the Genoese name Gianni; in the late 18th century 34% of the civilian male population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, and nearly 20% of Gibraltarian surnames are Italian in origin. Others link it to the English name Johnny, or to the plain language of ordinary Gibraltarians.</p><p>Several periods shaped it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>; after the Peninsular War British authorities set up an education system of British inspiration; during the evacuation of Gibraltar in the Second World War Spanish was relegated to a foreign language subject; and from 1969 to 1982 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> closed the land border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Andalusian Spanish from the surrounding Campo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> is its main constituent, heavily influenced by British English. It contains over 500 words from the medieval Genoese dialect of Ligurian, some words of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> origin through Judaeo-Spanish, and a Jewish influence from Haketia, and also draws on Maltese, Portuguese, Menorcan Catalan and Darija Arabic. Speakers switch between Spanish and English from sentence to sentence and mix them within sentences. It has no official orthography.</p><p>Examples include beki for bacon and quecaró for porridge, from the brand Quaker Oats, and the calque te llamo p'atrás for I'll call you back. The saying ¿El hijo del Melbil? alludes to Lord Melville, a British statesman of the early 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Llanito has been described as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>'s dying mother-tongue, since English is increasingly dominant and younger speakers use little of it. It is seldom written, but Manuel Cavilla published the Diccionario Yanito in 1978 and Tito Vallejo The Yanito Dictionary in 2001. The Gibraltar Broadcasting Corporation has broadcast programmes in it, among them Talk About Town and Pepe's Pot, and the documentary People of the Rock: The Llanitos of Gibraltar appeared in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Llanito” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its mixed population of Genoese, Spanish, Maltese, Jewish and British origin developed a distinct identity and the <b>Llanito</b> speech.</blockquote><a href="/state/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a></li></ul></section>
